## Blocking check finding round 1 did not mention

  traditio.present   opera/W-029.md   active item (building) has no handoff

This is a blocker naming opera/W-029.md. It is shared by all six building
opera, so it is an orchestration gap rather than a defect in this opus's
work, and I do not fail the opus for it. But it is not cleared by passing
review, and it is not cleared by closing either: `closeChecks`
(packages/cli/src/close.ts:14-31) gates only on state being one of
building/verifying/review — it consults neither the review probatio nor
`check`. Closing W-029 will leave `check studio --repo .` failing on
traditio.present unless a handoff is recorded first. Related: `wip.cap`,
6 in progress against a cap of 4.

## Scope — round 1's statement understates the commits

Round 1: "All three present and unchanged outside declared scope." True of
the test assets, not of the commits.

130f29a also touched .gitignore (three Playwright artifact dirs),
package-lock.json (the @playwright/test install), docs/SESSION-HANDOFF.md
and four dossier HTML files. 3ed05c8, the behaviour-4 fix, touched
apps/web/src/screens/Officina.tsx — outside the brief's Files owned.

Judged acceptable: the gitignore lines and the lockfile are the mechanical
consequence of the declared interface (`test:e2e` + the devDep), and the
Officina fix repairs a real W-026 regression that this suite caught, which
is the suite doing its job. Recorded, not waived silently.

Note for the collegium: qa.md §2 says "Blocks a write outside an opus's
declared files (check: probatio.declared)". That citation is wrong.
check.ts:398's probatio.declared only asserts a gate id appears in the
manifest; nothing in check compares written files against a brief's Files
owned. The clause is unenforced and is being applied by reviewer judgement,
which the standing rule "process enforcement is a check rule or a hook,
never a memory or prose" says it should not be.

## Concurrency observed during this review

At 22:15:30 +0800, packages/cli/src/rules/paths.ts appeared modified in the
working tree with `path.id.unvalidated` and `path.escapes.officina`
downgraded from "block" to "advise"; by 22:15:36 it was reverted. Not my
edit — a concurrent agent mutation-testing check's path rules in the same
checkout. My verify certificate was computed at 22:13, before it, and
`git status --porcelain apps/web` was clean throughout, so W-029's evidence
is unaffected.

Flagged anyway: this is class review×no-builder-isolation (L-003, L-010),
still recurring. Two agents mutating one shared working tree can have one
certify a tree that momentarily contained the other's mutation. That my
certificate landed in a clean window is luck, not isolation.

## Advisories carried forward or added

1. (round 1 #1, still true) No green log for `npm run test:e2e` exists in
   studio bookkeeping. Two reviews have now verified the acceptance
   criterion live and neither run is recorded as a probatio. Class
   review×evidence-backfilled-or-absent (L-001, L-008, L-017, L-021),
   already the studio's most recurrent class.
2. (round 1 #2, confirmed) Test 5's comment says "no full reload" and the
   test asserts nothing about reloading. A full reload on hash change would
   pass it. Comment overstates.
3. (new) playwright.config.ts uses a fixed port 5199 with
   `reuseExistingServer: !process.env["CI"]`. Locally, an unrelated server
   already on 5199 is silently reused and the suite asserts against the
   wrong app. Harmless in CI, a false green waiting to happen locally.

## Verdict

PASS. The opus does what the brief asked, the acceptance criterion passes
live on the current tree, the reds are honest (one of them caught a real
regression in someone else's work), the suite bites under an app-side
mutation, and tests/lint/types are re-certified against the tree as it
stands. Round 1 reached the right verdict on partly wrong reasoning.
